// docs_trading.dw with ONE line changed: approvals are forbidden rather than permitted.
//
// Nothing else moved. The SellShares permit below is byte-identical to the one in docs_trading.dw
// -- it still names ApproveSale, still validates, still reads like a working approval gate. And it
// now authorizes nothing at all, because an approval that policy denies is recorded as an `error`
// rather than a `response`, so the event it waits for is never written.
//
//     python tests/strands/vacuity.py tests/policies/docs_trading_forbidden.dw
//     ==> permit #2  action == SellShares    VACUOUS
//
// This is the shape of the problem worth caring about. The forbid is a reasonable thing for a
// security team to add -- tighten approvals, they are being abused -- and its author has no
// reason to read the trading rules. The permit's own author has no reason to re-read it either.
// Nothing in either file says they now interact, and no validator reports it.

forbid (
    principal,
    action == Anchor::Action::"ApproveSale",
    resource
);

permit (
    principal,
    action == Anchor::Action::"SellShares",
    resource
)
when temporal {
    formerly within 3600s Anchor::Action::"ApproveSale"::response{
        input.stock: context.input.stock,
        output.approved: true
    }
};
